COMMIT 261c6570e78137ffda54a1066d5bbd382decf3a2
test(core): FIX-123 pin the persist warning's sentence

`Trail.persist()` tells the operator the body could not be made durable and
that the message's rows are therefore recorded metadata-only. FIX-54 pinned
the `message <id>: ` prefix and FIX-121 fixed how the two cases filter the
captured records, but nothing asserted the sentence itself: the reviewer's
hand mutant for FIX-121 reworded it and all 13 cases stayed green.

Four assertions in the two persist cases, two per case, so each half of the
sentence is load-bearing on its own. Proved by one hand mutant per half: the
cause phrase mutated fails TrailTest:390/:412 while the tail still reads
correctly, the tail mutated fails :391/:413 while the cause phrase still
reads correctly. Neither assertion subsumes the other.

The cause text ("pg down") is deliberately not asserted: it is the fake
store's words, not Trail's, and pinning it would break this test whenever an
unrelated fixture changes its exception message.

PIT could not have found this gap — its default mutators do not alter string
literals — so a hand mutant is the only instrument for a message's wording.

